# Generic future expansions

1. Architecture decisions
   Add lightweight architecture decision records for important choices, alternatives, and consequences. This prevents future agents from reversing deliberate decisions.
2. Testing conventions
   Define preferences for unit, integration, API, UI, and end-to-end testing, including naming, fixtures, mocking, test data, and acceptable coverage.
3. Security conventions
   Cover authentication, authorization, secrets, sensitive logging, dependency risk, input validation, threat modelling, and secure defaults.
4. Data and persistence
   Document database selection, migrations, transactions, entity boundaries, retention, backups, caching, and concurrency preferences.
5. Observability
   Define logging structure, log levels, metrics, traces, correlation identifiers, health checks, and what must never be logged.
6. API contract design
   Add shared guidance for versioning, DTO compatibility, pagination, filtering, status codes, idempotency, and breaking-change management. This would complement project-specific pathing.md.
7. Delivery and releases
   Cover CI checks, deployment environments, versioning, changelogs, release notes, rollback expectations, and publishing workflows.
8. Performance principles
   Record expectations for measurement, profiling, caching, database queries, allocation, asynchronous work, startup time, and performance budgets.
9. Accessibility and product content
   Separate accessibility, interface copy, terminology, empty states, validation messages, and content tone from visual front-end design.
10. AI Brain maintenance
   Add automated checks for broken references, oversized descriptors, orphaned instruction indexes, duplicated routes, empty documentation, naming inconsistencies, and excessive routing depth.

